{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: the product of the digits","reason":"For digits a and b, (10a+b)²-(10b+a)²=99(a-b)(a+b). This supplies factors 9, 11, a+b, and a-b when the divisor is nonzero. For 12 and 21, the difference is -297, which is not divisible by the digit product 2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The digit-difference divisibility statement is understood for unequal digits. Equal digits give a zero difference and a zero proposed divisor, which elementary division does not define."],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p017","cnm_027","cnm_p153"],"id":"factor-digit-reversal-difference","label":"Expose the guaranteed factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-digit-reversal-difference":["cnm_155","cnm_152","cnm_071"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_155","cnm_152","cnm_071","cnm_p017","cnm_027","cnm_p153"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (0)","reason":"Let R_(n+1) be the number consisting of n+1 ones. The term with n ones on each side of the 2 equals (10^n+1)R_(n+1). For every n=1,...,10, both factors exceed 1, so all ten terms are composite.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_10B_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"overlapping-strings-of-ones","label":"Overlap two strings of ones","methodDirectNodeIds":{"overlapping-strings-of-ones":["cnm_p157","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p157","cnm_155"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (0)","reason":"The nth term has n ones on each side of its central 2 and equals ((10^(n+1)-1)/9)(10^n+1). Both factors are integers greater than one for every n≥1. Hence all first ten terms are composite, and none are prime.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12B_Q03","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"overlapping-strings-of-ones","label":"Overlap two strings of ones","methodDirectNodeIds":{"overlapping-strings-of-ones":["cnm_p157","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p157","cnm_155"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"At least C and D are valid proof forms.","reason":"C proves divisibility by 8 because both a(a+1) and b(b+1) are even. D also proves it: the two factors a-b and a+b+1 have odd sum 2a+1 and therefore opposite parity, so their product is even and 4(a-b)(a+b+1) is divisible by 8. The stem does not ask for the most direct particular textbook form.","status":"confirmed_ambiguous"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["The choices are algebraically equivalent, and D also supplies a direct parity proof. The wording does not uniquely require the consecutive-product argument displayed in C."],"questionId":"1954_AMC_12_Q49","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_148","cnm_146","cnm_p156","cnm_p153"],"id":"consecutive-products","label":"Consecutive-integer products","methodDirectNodeIds":{"consecutive-products":["cnm_155","cnm_151"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_155","cnm_151","cnm_148","cnm_146","cnm_p156","cnm_p153"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_146","cnm_p156","cnm_p153"],"id":"factored-parity","label":"Factor the difference of squares","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factored-parity":["cnm_155","cnm_152"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_155","cnm_152","cnm_146","cnm_p156","cnm_p153"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 0","reason":"The sum factors as 7^2024(1+7+49)=57·7^2024. Since 57=3·19, the remainder upon division by 19 is 0.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10B_Q07","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_142"],"id":"factor-common-power","label":"Factor out the common power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-common-power":["cnm_151","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_142","cnm_155","cnm_151"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 802","reason":"Factor f(n) = (n^2 + 20n + 20)(n^2 - 20n + 20). The first factor exceeds 1 for every positive integer n, so a positive prime value requires n^2 - 20n + 20 = 1. This gives (n - 10)^2 = 81, hence n = 1 or 19. The corresponding values are the primes 41 and 761, whose sum is 802.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12B_Q19","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_029","cnm_147","cnm_150"],"id":"factor-and-restrict-prime-values","label":"Factor the expression and use primality","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-restrict-prime-values":["cnm_155","cnm_p157","cnm_148","cnm_152","cnm_221","cnm_220"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_155","cnm_p157","cnm_148","cnm_152","cnm_221","cnm_220","cnm_p153","cnm_029","cnm_147","cnm_150"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 37","reason":"If H is the sum of the hundreds digits, the tens and units digits also sum to H. Hence S = 100H+10H+H = 111H = 3·37H. The valid cycle (123, 231, 312) sums to 666 = 2·3^2·37, which has no prime factor larger than 37.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_10A_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p055","cnm_p114","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"],"id":"compare-digit-column-sums","label":"Compare the three digit-column sums","methodDirectNodeIds":{"compare-digit-column-sums":["cnm_155","math:prime-factorization"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_155","math:prime-factorization","cnm_p055","cnm_p114","cnm_p153","cnm_p157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A","reason":"Let k=(p-1)/2, a positive integer. Since p-1 is congruent to 1 modulo p-2, (p-1)^k-1 is divisible by p-2. Equivalently, it equals (p-2)(1+(p-1)+...+(p-1)^(k-1)).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1967_AMC_12_Q25","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"finite-geometric-factorization","label":"Use a finite geometric sum","nodeIds":["cnm_h210","cnm_155"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_p156","cnm_019","cnm_145"],"id":"solution:finite-geometric-factorization:7f57eafed8b8","label":"Exhibit an integer factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"finite-geometric-factorization":["cnm_155","cnm_h210"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_155","cnm_h210","cnm_p153","cnm_p156","cnm_019","cnm_145"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, one positive integer","reason":"The expression factors as (n-1)(n-2). For n=1 or 2 it is 0; for n=3 it is the prime 2; and for n≥4 both factors exceed 1, making the product composite. Thus only n=3 qualifies.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_12B_Q03","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"consecutive-factor-primality","label":"Inspect consecutive factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"consecutive-factor-primality":["cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_155"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_029","cnm_146"],"id":"consecutive-factors","label":"Expose the factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"consecutive-factors":["cnm_p157","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p157","cnm_155","cnm_p153","cnm_029","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 63 and 65","reason":"Because 12 divides 48, 2^12-1 divides 2^48-1. Also 2^12-1 = (64-1)(64+1) = 63*65, so both proposed numbers divide the given integer.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1971_AMC_12_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"factor-power-difference","label":"Factor differences of powers","nodeIds":["cnm_152","cnm_155"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_143","cnm_147"],"id":"solution:factor-power-difference:269aafbb9613","label":"Factor toward the nearby power of 2","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-power-difference":["cnm_p153","cnm_152","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_152","cnm_155","cnm_143","cnm_147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 42","reason":"Let the ages be d,t,h. Substituting t = 2h - 3d into the cubic equation gives 6(h - d)²(h - 4d) = 0. The branch h = d makes t negative, so h = 4d and t = 5d. Relative primality gives d = 1, hence 1² + 5² + 4² = 42.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":["Ages are interpreted as positive integers, as required by the relative-primality condition."],"questionId":"1972_AMC_12_Q34","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"verify-cubic-expansion","label":"Expand and verify the factored homogeneous cubic","methodDirectNodeIds":{"verify-cubic-expansion":["cnm_146","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_146","cnm_155"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 4","reason":"For every integer a, a³-a=(a-1)a(a+1) is divisible by 6. Therefore the requested sum is congruent to 2018^2018≡2^2018≡4 mod 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_10B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cube-congruence-and-power-cycle","label":"Reduce cubes and use a power cycle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cube-congruence-and-power-cycle":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","math:modular-powers","cnm_155"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 2^607 - 1","reason":"For choice C, the residues modulo 2, 3, 5, and 7 are respectively 1, 1, 2, and 1, all nonzero. The other choices have small prime divisors: A and D are divisible by 3, while B and E are divisible by 5.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_10B_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-to-eliminate","label":"Find factors in four choices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-to-eliminate":["cnm_155","cnm_p157"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_155","cnm_p157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C. 2^607 - 1","reason":"The residues of 2^607 - 1 modulo 2, 3, 5, and 7 are respectively 1, 1, 2, and 1. Meanwhile, A is divisible by 3, B by 5, D by 3, and E by 2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12B_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-to-eliminate","label":"Find factors in four choices","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-to-eliminate":["cnm_155","cnm_p157"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_155","cnm_p157"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 20; K = 758","reason":"The numeral equals 2b³+2b+4, so divisibility by 16 is equivalent to b³+b+2 ≡ 0 (mod 8). Testing residues modulo 8 gives b ≡ 3,6,7. Each class occurs 253 times in 1 through 2024, but b=3 must be excluded. Thus K=3·253-1=758, and its digit sum is 20.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_10A_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-and-check-residues","label":"Factor and check remainders","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-check-residues":["cnm_024","math:modular-congruence","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_024","math:modular-congruence","cnm_155"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 20","reason":"Divisibility is equivalent to b³+b+2 being 0 modulo 8. Testing the eight residues gives b congruent to 3, 6, or 7. Each occurs 253 times from 1 through 2024; excluding bases below 5 removes only b=3. Thus K=758 and its digit sum is 7+5+8=20.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2024_AMC_12A_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-and-count-residues","label":"Factor and count remainder classes","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-count-residues":["cnm_024","math:modular-congruence","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_024","math:modular-congruence","cnm_155"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"1400","reason":"57^2-43^2=(57-43)(57+43)=14*100=1400; direct subtraction gives 3249-1849=1400.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_155_q1","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"factor-numerical-square-difference","label":"Use a difference of squares","nodeIds":["cnm_152","cnm_155"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 6","reason":"Since n^3-n=(n-1)n(n+1), it contains an even factor and a multiple of 3, so 6 always divides it. At n=2 the expression equals 6, ruling out any larger universal divisor.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1951_AMC_12_Q15","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"consecutive-integer-factors","label":"Factor into consecutive integers","nodeIds":["cnm_151","cnm_152","cnm_155"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_150","cnm_147","cnm_p156"],"id":"solution:consecutive-integer-factors:b19008412df8","label":"Factor into consecutive integers","methodDirectNodeIds":{"consecutive-integer-factors":["cnm_151","cnm_152","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_151","cnm_152","cnm_155","cnm_150","cnm_147","cnm_p156"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"600","reason":"3x^2-3y^2=3(x-y)(x+y)=3*(51-49)*(51+49)=3*2*100=600.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"cn-math","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"cnm_154_q3","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"factor-before-evaluation","label":"Factor before substituting","nodeIds":["cnm_151","cnm_152","cnm_155"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_147"],"id":"solution:factor-before-evaluation:8881fe8173b7","label":"Factor before substituting","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-before-evaluation":["cnm_151","cnm_152","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_151","cnm_152","cnm_155","cnm_147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, 2 pairs","reason":"The equation is equivalent to (m-1)(n-1)=1. Its integer factor pairs are (1,1) and (-1,-1), yielding exactly (m,n)=(2,2) and (0,0).","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_146","cnm_014","cnm_040"],"id":"complete-a-product","label":"Turn the equation into a product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"complete-a-product":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_155","cnm_146","cnm_014","cnm_040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B. One positive integer","reason":"The expression factors as (n-1)(n-2). It is zero for n=1,2, equals the prime 2 for n=3, and is a product of two integers greater than 1 for every n>=4. Thus only n=3 works.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_10B_Q06","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_150","cnm_p153"],"id":"factor-and-classify","label":"Expose the factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-classify":["cnm_151","cnm_155","cnm_p157"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_151","cnm_155","cnm_p157","cnm_150","cnm_p153"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 5/3","reason":"Since 2^2014 = 4 × 2^2012, the quotient becomes [2^2012(4 + 1)]/[2^2012(4 - 1)] = 5/3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2013_AMC_10A_Q08","reviewStatus":"machine_examined","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_142","cnm_p107"],"id":"common-power-factor","label":"Factor out the smaller power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"common-power-factor":["cnm_021","cnm_155","cnm_151"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_021","cnm_155","cnm_151","cnm_142","cnm_p107"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 12","reason":"Write the expression as n²(n-1)(n+1). One of the three consecutive integers is divisible by 3. If n is even, 4 divides n²; if n is odd, both neighbors are even. For n=2 the expression is 12, so divisibility by 24 is not guaranteed.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1956_AMC_12_Q34","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_147"],"id":"solution:factor-and-use-parity:462e2ffab28e","label":"Find factors of three and four","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-use-parity":["cnm_155","cnm_152","cnm_p156","cnm_p159"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_155","cnm_152","cnm_p156","cnm_p159","cnm_p153","cnm_147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E: 30","reason":"For every integer n, n^5-n is divisible by 2, 3, and 5, hence by 30. At n=2 the value is exactly 30, so no larger positive integer can divide every term.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1957_AMC_12_Q32","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_151","cnm_152","cnm_p159"],"id":"factor-and-check-divisibility","label":"Factor and check divisibility","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-check-divisibility":["cnm_p153","cnm_155","math:modular-congruence"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_155","math:modular-congruence","cnm_151","cnm_152","cnm_p159"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E. 17","reason":"The consecutive factors always provide a factor of 2. A factor of 3 is absent exactly when s is divisible by 3. There are 9 such integers from 0 through 25, leaving 26-9=17.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1964_AMC_12_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_p156","cnm_146"],"id":"factor-and-count","label":"Factor and count the exceptions","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-count":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_155","cnm_h010"]},"nodeIds":["math:modular-congruence","cnm_155","cnm_h010","cnm_p153","cnm_p156","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":5,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 16","reason":"Let the integer roots be r and s. Vieta gives r+s=a and rs=2a, hence (r-2)(s-2)=4. Up to interchange, the integer factor pairs (1,4), (2,2), (-1,-4), and (-2,-2) give a=9, 8, -1, and 0. Their sum is 16.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2015_AMC_10A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h049","cnm_p153","cnm_146"],"id":"integer-root-factor-pairs","label":"Relate the integer roots by a product","methodDirectNodeIds":{"integer-root-factor-pairs":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_226","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_226","cnm_155","cnm_h049","cnm_p153","cnm_146"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, always","reason":"The factors p - 1 and p + 1 are consecutive even integers, so their product is divisible by 8. Since p is not divisible by 3, one of them is divisible by 3. Therefore 24 divides (p - 1)(p + 1) = p² - 1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1973_AMC_12_Q18","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_p153","cnm_p157","cnm_p156","cnm_147"],"id":"neighboring-factors","label":"Examine the neighboring factors","methodDirectNodeIds":{"neighboring-factors":["cnm_155","cnm_152","cnm_p159"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_155","cnm_152","cnm_p159","cnm_p153","cnm_p157","cnm_p156","cnm_147"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A (6)","reason":"For legs x≤y and hypotenuse z, the condition gives inradius 6, hence x+y-z=12. Pythagoras then yields (x-12)(y-12)=72, with x,y>12. The six unordered positive factor pairs are (1,72), (2,36), (3,24), (4,18), (6,12), and (8,9). Each produces a valid triangle, so the answer is 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2007_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_p153","cnm_147","cnm_148"],"id":"eliminate-hypotenuse-and-count-factors","label":"Eliminate the hypotenuse","methodDirectNodeIds":{"eliminate-hypotenuse-and-count-factors":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_155"]},"nodeIds":["math:integer-diophantine-equations","cnm_155","cnm_174","cnm_p148","cnm_p153","cnm_147","cnm_148"]}]}],"layer":"ailleo","meaning":"Reviewed content-bound concept practice; no claim of complete source-standard coverage or learner mastery.","nextOffset":null,"nodeId":"cnm_155","status":"mapped","total":28}
